ReaperPy
========

ReaperPy itself is released under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

It builds on ideas, file-format knowledge and protocol details from the projects below.  ReaperPy's code is its own
implementation; no source files were copied unchanged.  Where a part follows the original in content, the file header
says so.

ps5debug-NG payload
-------------------
  Author:   OSR (OpenSourcereR); maintained and extended by Pharaoh2k (with EchoStretch and Sonic-Iso)
  Source:   https://github.com/Pharaoh2k/ps5debug-NG
  License:  GNU General Public License v3.0

  ReaperPy is a client for this payload and cannot do anything on the console without it.  It talks to the payload over
  TCP (command port 744, debugger back channel 755, kernel log 3232) according to PROTOCOL.md (v1.3.2) and uses its
  commands for process memory, scans, the debugger, disassembly, function calls and on-screen notifications.
  No source code, binaries or other files of ps5debug-NG are part of ReaperPy; the payload is a separate program that you
  load onto your console yourself and that stays under its own license (GPL-3.0).  ReaperPy only implements the client
  side of the published wire protocol.

  ps5debug-NG itself is inspired by "ps5debug" by Ctn and SiSTRo and builds on "ps4debug" by jogolden, and it uses the
  ps5-payload-sdk by John Toernblom (https://github.com/ps5-payload-dev/sdk).  Thanks to all of them: without their
  work there would be no protocol to write a client for.

PS5Debug-studio
---------------
  Author:   alfizari
  Source:   https://github.com/alfizari/PS5Debug-studio
  License:  Apache License 2.0  (text: licenses/Apache-2.0.txt)

  ReaperPy has its own architecture and its own code.  From PS5Debug-studio only a few tweaks (small ideas and
  format/protocol details, no code) were taken over:
    cheatfmt.py         SHN XML layout (Trainer/Cheat/Cheatline); .mc4 = SHN + AES-256-CBC + base64 with the publicly
                        known mc4 key/IV; file names TITLEID_VERSION.ext
    ps5_more.py         call trampoline (stack offset for PROC_CALL), layout of the FOREGROUND (0xBDDD0006),
                        WRITE_MULTI (0xBDAACC04) and PTRSCAN (0xBDAACC30) commands
    ptrscan_server.py   "the console scans level by level" method (backwards breadth-first search)
    logger.py           idea: log function calls through a code cave
    tools_extra_gui.py  idea: function caller / function logger as separate tabs

  Changes against the original (Apache-2.0 section 4b): everything was rewritten in Python and built into ReaperPy's
  own architecture (tabs, backend, patch engine).

ps4-unreal-dumper
-----------------
  Author:   TetzkatLipHoka  (uediff/peek: Stoned)
  Source:   https://github.com/TetzkatLipHoka/ps4-unreal-dumper
  License:  MIT

  The UE features of ReaperPy read the output of this dumper (dump.bin, dump.json, dump.bin.engine.json,
  SDK/offsets.json, SDK/GObjects.txt, SDK/SDK_HEADERS/*).  Calling a UFunction through ProcessEvent (ue_call.py)
  follows the method of `uecall`.  No code was copied - only formats and approach.  Dumper-7 (Encryqed) is the basis of
  the object model there.

Debugger behaviour
------------------
  debugger.py re-implements the stop / continue / step flow (server-managed breakpoint step, live-register check after
  a hit, deferred breakpoint removal while stopped) in ReaperPy's own code.  It was modelled on the observable
  behaviour of the closed-source "PS5 Debug Toolkit"; no code, assets or binaries of that program are included.

Third-party packages (installed with pip, not part of this repository)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  capstone         BSD-3-Clause   disassembler
  keystone-engine  GPL-2.0        assembler
  Pillow           HPND           images (icon, splash screen)

  IMPORTANT for binary builds: build_exe.bat / ReaperPy.spec (PyInstaller) bundle these packages into the EXE.  The
  source repository does not contain them, but a distributed EXE does.  keystone-engine is GPL-2.0: before you
  distribute a build, check what that means for the whole bundle (this is not legal advice).
